  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA low pressure system is still moving through the region, after\nyielding locally heavy rainfall in parts of the area including\nsome flash flooding Sunday evening. Though the axis of heavier\nrainfall will shunt generally east of the area with time, until\nthen we could still have isolated heavy rain where repeated \nstripes of more robust showers can line up.\n\nWhile the focus in the first phase of this system has been on \nthe hydro side of things, there will also still be a marginal \nrisk for a few thunderstorms with gusty winds from the Poconos \nto the southern Catskills today. Mixed Layer Convective \nAvailable Potential Energy (MLCAPE) values increase to slightly \nabove 500 J/kg over Poconos-Catskills while exiting east around\nmid- afternoon, with 30-50 knots west-southwesterly flow in the\n2-6km layer pointing to a decent amount of shear.\n\nHigh pressure builds in for a brief reprieve from the wet \nweather for generally dry conditions Tuesday and most if not all\nof Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nLate Wednesday into Wednesday night, warm air advection regime\nwith one or two weak waves could cause a few showers and\nisolated thunder, but our next main rain chances will focus on \nThursday through Friday for several reasons. The Bermuda high \nis expected to shift west, drawing up plenty of very warm and \nhumid air from the south and driving up dew points. Meanwhile a \nbroad upper trough over the northern Plains, Upper Midwest and \nGreat Lakes will channel waves of energy toward our region and a\nweakening cut off upper low over the Tennessee Valley looks to \ngradually drift northeast across the southern and central \nAppalachians later in the week. The next frontal boundary also \nlooks to try to approach from the west in the Thursday-Friday \ntime frame and perhaps stall a bit. \n\nSo putting everything together, there will be multiple chances \nfor showers and storms with dew points anywhere from the mid 60s\nto the mid 70s in spots. This will lead to some uncomfortable \nmuggy conditions with high temperatures mainly in the 80s each \nafternoon and heat index values probably reaching at or above 90\nin lower elevations. With Precipitable Water (Pwat) values \naround or exceeding 1.75 inches, we will again have to monitor \npotential for locally heavy rainfall, and if waves are well-\ntimed diurnally perhaps a few stronger thunderstorms as well.",
